{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 3869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Have you ever asked “what is beyond the\nedge of the universe?”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3989,
    "dDurationMs": 5051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And have you ever been told that an infinite\nuniverse that has no edge?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You were told wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10040,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a sense."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11700,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can define a boundary to an infinite universe,\nat least mathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16720,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it turns out that boundary may be as real\nor even more real than the universe it contains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29120,
    "dDurationMs": 1880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe may be infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31010,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to wrap our puny human minds around\nsuch a notion we like to come up with boundaries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37710,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example we have the “observable universe”\n– that patch that we can see, and beyond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43360,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which light has not yet had time to reach\nus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46320,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s boundary is called the particle horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50010,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beyond it there exists at a minimum of thousands\nand possibly infinitely more regions just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55600,
    "dDurationMs": 1779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as large."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57379,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our observable universe is like a tiny patch\nof land in a vast plain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62739,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We define its horizon like we might build\na little picket fence around our little patch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68020,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– meaningless from the point of view of\nthe plain, but it makes our patch feel more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73040,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "homey and us less crushingly insignificant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77740,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We visited these cosmic horizons in one of\nthe early Space Time episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82950,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To review: the particle horizon defines the\nlimit of the visible past, and there’s also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89840,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic event horizon defining the limit of\nthe visible future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94430,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these correspond to actual spherical boundaries\nin space, whose distances can be calculated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100670,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How mundane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102590,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s another way to define the boundary\nof the universe that isn’t so shy in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107890,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "face of an infinite cosmos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110530,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, if we twist our human intuition and\nour mathematics to its limit we can build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116910,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our picket fence around an infinite universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120090,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A mathematical boundary at infinity turns\nout to be not just useful for doing calculations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126340,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in physics, but may be as real as the physical\nuniverse it contains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131160,
    "dDurationMs": 4299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may encode that universe as a hologram\non its surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135459,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In today’s episode we’re going to talk\nabout two ways to define an infinite boundary,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141239,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and this will set us up finally for laying\nopen the holographic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145109,
    "dDurationMs": 1311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I promise!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146420,
    "dDurationMs": 3329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with a quick review of types\nof universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149749,
    "dDurationMs": 5591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least the three basic types described by\nEinstein’s general theory of relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155340,
    "dDurationMs": 5399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only type of non-infinite universe – or\nclosed universe – is the one that curves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160739,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back on itself – like a 3-D analog of the\n2-D surface of a sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166049,
    "dDurationMs": 2401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call that positive curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168450,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Travel far enough and you get back where you\nstarted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172790,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geometry is a bit broken in such a universe\n– for example, two parallel lines will eventually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177159,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converge and cross each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180219,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general relativity we call a universe with\nthis geometry de Sitter space, after Dutch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186359,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomer Willem de Sitter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188139,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then there’s the flat universe – classic,\nstraightforward geometry – parallel lines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194079,
    "dDurationMs": 3231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stay parallel – and it goes on forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197310,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In GR this is Minkowski space, after Hermann\nMinkowski, teacher and colleague of Albert"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203500,
    "dDurationMs": 1529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205029,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally there’s the universe with negative\ncurvature, and the 2-D analog of that is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210709,
    "dDurationMs": 5441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperbolic surface, like an infinite saddle\nor pringle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216150,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this geometry parallel lines actually diverge\nfrom each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220319,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In GR this is anti-de Sitter space because\nit’s the “opposite” of the positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226409,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature de Sitter space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228889,
    "dDurationMs": 2921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Terrible name, so we abbreviate it AdS space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231810,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so 2 out of 3 possible universes are infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237340,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, assuming all these types\nexist, there should be infinitely more people"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242459,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in infinite universes compared to people in\nnon-infinite universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247530,
    "dDurationMs": 6609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re probably one of the former, so let’s\nignore puny de Sitter space and for today"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254139,
    "dDurationMs": 2841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assume we’re in one of the infinite ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256980,
    "dDurationMs": 3819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we put bounds on infinity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260799,
    "dDurationMs": 6811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all got going in the early 60s when physicists\ntried to find ways to map infinite spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267610,
    "dDurationMs": 5089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "–to the edge of an infinite universe or\nacross the event horizon of a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272699,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regular coordinates of space and time are\nuseless there – they blow up to infinities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278860,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicists found mathematical ways to fuse\nspace and time into new coordinates that suppressed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284770,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the infinities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286360,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call this process compactification."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289680,
    "dDurationMs": 5849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first efforts were designed to allow physicists\nto cross the event horizon of black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295529,
    "dDurationMs": 2031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– mathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have Kruskal–Szekeres coordinates, Eddington-Finkelstein\ncoordinates, and others."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303680,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these coordinates only defeated the artificial\ninfinity – the coordinate singularity - of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309900,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon – also something we’ve\ndiscussed (the phantom singularity)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313560,
    "dDurationMs": 5949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was Roger Penrose who defeated the true\ninfinity of an infinitely large universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319509,
    "dDurationMs": 5861,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the early 60s he developed his Penrose\ncoordinates and Penrose diagrams – also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325370,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known as Penrose-Carter diagrams, for Brandon\nCarter who came up with them around the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330819,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331819,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve used these before to understand black\nhole event horizons, but these were originally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336479,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conceived to understand the boundaries of\nthe universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340199,
    "dDurationMs": 7771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a quick review: start with a graph of space\nversus time – a spacetime diagram – then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347970,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compactify."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349610,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These horizontal-ish contours are our old\ntime ticks - moments of constant time across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe, while the vertical-ish lines\nare set locations in space in only one spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361920,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363210,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The contours bunch up towards the boundaries\nso that every step on the map covers more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368400,
    "dDurationMs": 2009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and more space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370409,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The boundaries themselves represent infinite\ndistance and infinite past and future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376710,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One amazing thing about the Penrose diagram\nis that the transformation preserves all internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383130,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles – all angles between intersecting\nlines relative to each other stay the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388830,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call such a transformation “conformal”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392669,
    "dDurationMs": 3101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that word – it’s going to be\nvery, very important."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395770,
    "dDurationMs": 5679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This particular conformal compactification\nis designed to ensure that the path of every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401449,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ray of light remains at 45 degrees across\nthe map."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406669,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means only lightspeed paths can hope\nto reach these boundaries ahead, and only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412629,
    "dDurationMs": 4241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light speed paths can originate from these\nboundaries behind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416870,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any sub-lightspeed paths, which means anything\nwith mass, will be swept along with the contours"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423580,
    "dDurationMs": 1489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425069,
    "dDurationMs": 6461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All matter must originate at this point representing\nall of space in the infinite past, and must"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431530,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also converge to this point which represents\nall of space in the infinite future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436970,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only lightspeed paths – or in the language\nof quantum field theory “massless fields”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442689,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can access these diagonal boundaries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445669,
    "dDurationMs": 5891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we write the equations of these fields\nin Penrose’s compactified coordinates then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451560,
    "dDurationMs": 6359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can do something that seems impossible\n– we can track a quantum field to infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457919,
    "dDurationMs": 3611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance and calculate its behavior there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461530,
    "dDurationMs": 2669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That has a very particular use."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464199,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose diagrams represent a universe that\nis “asymptotically flat” – it may have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470219,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some local curvature due to gravity of massive\nobjects inside, but at its boundaries the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476099,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple rules of non-curved, Minkowski spacetime\napply."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481349,
    "dDurationMs": 7761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s handy because flat space is the only\nspace where quantum mechanics is fully solvable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489110,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most famous use of this is by Steven Hawking,\nas we saw in our Hawking radiation episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495590,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He connected a quantum field between two points\nat infinite distance – past and future - where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501830,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he could define the state of the quantum vacuum\nin solvable flat space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506590,
    "dDurationMs": 6449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then he placed a black hole in between these\npoints and calculated how it perturbed the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513039,
    "dDurationMs": 4281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balance of a quantum field traced between\nthem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517320,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He found that two “infinitely distant”\nregions could not both be in a perfect vacuum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522190,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state if a black hole lay between them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524970,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He concluded that the black hole must generate\nparticles – Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529790,
    "dDurationMs": 6341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which, by the way, was a key discovery on\nthe path to the holographic principle, as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536131,
    "dDurationMs": 5258,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve discussed before and which I’ll\n. review again - but not today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541389,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re just talking about boundaries\n– and we need a very different infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546870,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "boundary to give us our hologram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549510,
    "dDurationMs": 7269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose diagrams define the infinite boundary\nof a flat universe as a useful tool in calculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556779,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the holographic principle we need the\ninfinite boundary of a negatively-curved universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562209,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– an anti-de Sitter, AdS universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566440,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with another map."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569110,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like the Penrose diagram we’ll do a\nconformal transformation – all internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573910,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles preserved, and will compactify layers\ntowards the edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578480,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this time we’re not mapping space versus\ntime – we’ll just map two dimensions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583810,
    "dDurationMs": 1899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperbolic space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585709,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, let’s use the most famous conformal\ncompactification of hyperbolic space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591230,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s right, there is a most famous conformal\ncompactification of hyperbolic space, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596420,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ve probably seen it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598600,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is M.C."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600110,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Escher’s Circle Limit IV – the final in\na series of woodcuts inspired by a projection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605660,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of hyperbolic plane called the Poincaré or\nconformal disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611730,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The basic construction is straightforward\nenough – start with a circle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615709,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now fill it with a set of circle segments\nthat all intersect the circumference at right"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620970,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622350,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those circle segments represent the straight\nlines of a hyperbolic geometry projected onto"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628500,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the disk."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630000,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can see the hyperbolic behavior these arcs\n– they are geodesics – the straightest-possible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635750,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "paths in the geometry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637890,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any two paths that are parallel at one point\nwill diverge from each other in either direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644140,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a conformal transformation of a hyperbolic\nsurface because the angles of intersection"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649870,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these lines are preserved, and it’s compactified\nbecause an infinite hyperbolic surface fits"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656570,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a finite disk, with lengths represented\nby shorter and shorter arcs towards the rim."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662570,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other feature of a conformal mapping is\nthat shapes are preserved – at least locally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see that when we fill the circle with a\nregular choice of circle segments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674680,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They define a set of enclosed shapes that\nvary in size but not in shape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680470,
    "dDurationMs": 7419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an example of tessellation – tiling\na space with regular repeated shapes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687889,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hyperbolic space is fascinating because there\nare literally infinite ways it can be tessellated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693260,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with regular polygons, while spheres and flat\nspace each have only a small finite number"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698980,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of possible tessellations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700850,
    "dDurationMs": 6099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this disk can represent an infinite anti-de\nSitter universe with 2 spatial dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706949,
    "dDurationMs": 2441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a single instant in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709390,
    "dDurationMs": 6689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each tile represent the same size region of\nspace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716079,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The boundary is infinitely far away and looks\nthe same no matter where we travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721340,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we wanted to represent a 3-D AdS universe\nwe could use a Poincaré ball instead."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727080,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the real power of AdS space isn’t the\ncool art you can do with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732330,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least to physicists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734350,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the nature of the infinite boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736810,
    "dDurationMs": 9100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s a mouthful: the boundary of a conformally-compactified\nanti-de Sitter space is itself a conformally-compactified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745910,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Minkowski space with one fewer dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749220,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Got it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750240,
    "dDurationMs": 2029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cool, we’d done here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752269,
    "dDurationMs": 1751,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, let’s unpack that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754020,
    "dDurationMs": 4309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact let’s add the dimension of time\nto our hyperbolic projection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758329,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stack a bunch of Poincare disks – each representing\nan instant in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763070,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They give you a cylinder and representing\nan AdS spacetime with 2 spatial and one temporal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768670,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensions – let’s call that 2+1 dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773250,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand the surface of the disk\nhas only one dimension of space – the circumference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778519,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– and the same one-D of time – 1+1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781960,
    "dDurationMs": 1129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783089,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it turns out that the surface of the cylinder\n– which exists only in the compactified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788790,
    "dDurationMs": 8310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coordinates of the interior volume - is mathematically\nexactly a flat, Minkowski space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797100,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can extrapolate to any number of extra\ndimensions – say a 3+1 dimensional - Poincare"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804040,
    "dDurationMs": 7170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compactify it so you the infinite boundary\nbecomes a surface – that surface is a 2+1"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811210,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Minkowski plain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812610,
    "dDurationMs": 5169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the crazy thing is that you can treat\nthat surface space and the interior space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817779,
    "dDurationMs": 5261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– also called the “bulk” - as separate\nspacetimes with their own physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823040,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they are connected."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825660,
    "dDurationMs": 4869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every point on the flat surface maps to a\nset of paths through the hyperbolic interior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830529,
    "dDurationMs": 2691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– remember those circle arcs?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833220,
    "dDurationMs": 3679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patterns on the surface define the structure\nof interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836899,
    "dDurationMs": 6281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1997 Argentinian physicist Juan Maldacena\nfound an incredible correspondence between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843180,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these spaces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844720,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He realized that if you define a conformal\nquantum field theory in a 3+1-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850519,
    "dDurationMs": 7550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Minkowski space, that corresponded to an interesting\nmathematical structure in the enclosed 4+1-D"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858069,
    "dDurationMs": 2301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AdS space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860370,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That structure looked exactly like a string\ntheory with gravity and everything."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the AdS/CFT correspondence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871339,
    "dDurationMs": 6101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics in the form of a conformal\nfield theory in one space is a theory of quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877440,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity in a space with one higher dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880750,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hologram part is because the lower dimensional\nspace can be thought of as the infinitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886050,
    "dDurationMs": 3729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distant boundary of the higher dimensional\nspace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889779,
    "dDurationMs": 6211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every particle, every gravitational effect\nin the bulk is represented by quantum fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895990,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on an infinitely distant surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897770,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, I’m going to have to cut us off here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901180,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The deeply abstract relationship between these\ntwo spaces needs an entire episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906800,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stay tuned for the final installment of the\nholographic principle in not-so-infinitely-distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913410,
    "dDurationMs": 3169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916579,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we enjoyed another potential end\nof the universe when we talked about the Big"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920839,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rip - in which space tears itself to shreds\non subatomic scales due to runaway increase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926079,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927639,
    "dDurationMs": 4721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your excitement at our possible doom really\nshowed in your comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932360,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you asked what happens to black holes\nin the big rip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936440,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's... a great question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939170,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I thought that the answer was that black\nholes would be eroded into nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945129,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, if space is expanding faster than\nlight at the event horizon, that should counter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950709,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the light-speed flow of space into the event\nhorizon, causing the event horizon to shrink"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955579,
    "dDurationMs": 2271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the black hole to dissolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957850,
    "dDurationMs": 3489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some internet physicists agree with my intuition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961339,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I also read an interesting take by Alan\nRominger on physics stackexchange, in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966290,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he suggests that the shrinking cosmological\nhorizon could merge with the event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973310,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to produce a global state where everything\njust looks like an inflationary spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978920,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chris Hanline wisely notes that dark energy\nappears to break the conservation of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985540,
    "dDurationMs": 3909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's sort of true - except that the law\nof conservation of energy has a very clear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989449,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range of validity - it's valid in systems\nthat are time symmetric - systems where the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995410,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "global properties of the spacetime don't evolve\nover time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999470,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact conservation of energy comes from\nthis symmetry, as revealed by Noether's theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005360,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However our universe on its largest scales\nis not time symmetric - it's expanding, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011079,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past looks very different to the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013209,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In its most familiar form, conservation of\nenergy doesn't apply and so dark energy CAN"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018759,
    "dDurationMs": 2601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be created from nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021360,
    "dDurationMs": 6309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some have argued that energy is conserved\nand that dark energy is created from the increasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027669,
    "dDurationMs": 6371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative potential energy of the cosmic gravitational\nfield, but I think at that level this is all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1034040,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just different interpretations of the math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036750,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Swole Kot asks if the last months in the big\nrip scenario would be a painful and horrible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042240,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience for any sentient life still around\nat that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046150,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it wouldn't be great, that's for sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049490,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After some millions of years watching the\ngalaxies fall apart, the last phase of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053910,
    "dDurationMs": 3509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "destruction of the solar system would happen\npretty fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057419,
    "dDurationMs": 5561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There's going to be an unpleasant period between\nthe destruction of planet sized things and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062980,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the destruction of atom sized things where\nyou have the destruction of people-sized things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069430,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I guess the painful part would be when\nexpansion is fast enough that it starts to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074320,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disrupt the way chemistry works, without actually\nripping matter apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1078780,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our bodies are pretty dependent on chemistry\nworking normally, so there would be some minutes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084230,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hours of bad times as our molecules start\nto betray us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089330,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make matters worse, the Earth would be\nfalling apart at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093010,
    "dDurationMs": 6940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's like 2012 meets World War Z meets Chronicles\nof Riddick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099950,
    "dDurationMs": 8580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Guys, I think we just sold a movie."
    } ]
  } ]
}
